{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788129956,"nonce":"UtOIcW97mhmXM8FkMXp8pPXNrLw=","message":"OPEN-QUESTION RESOLUTIONS — 2.6 worker (opus), dorian's Linux box, 2026-08-30.\nBranch `spec/2.6-observer-remediation`. None of these changes another spec's\nshipped contract; where one touches a contract a LATER spec will read, that is\nsaid explicitly so it can be checked rather than assumed.\n\n1. FOG on `reachable` and `path-cost` — REPORT, do not refuse.\n\nComment #1 names four verbs \"alike\" (map-view, find-rect, nearest, room-at) and\nthose four now hide undiscovered cells outright. `reachable` and `path-cost`\ndeliberately do NOT: they publish `from_fogged` / `to_fogged` and answer\nnormally. The reason is the amendment's own test — \"information no player has\".\nA player cannot query reachability at all; they issue a move order and the pawn\npaths INTO fog like any other pawn, and the game computes that path through\nundiscovered ground. Refusing here would make the agent weaker than a player,\nnot equal to one, and would break the exploration behaviour fog exists to\nprovoke.\n\nBut WHAT is in the way is not player-obtainable, and the first cut of the\nblocker field leaked it: `reachable {to: \u003cfogged cell\u003e}` answered\n`{\"def\":\"Limestone\",\"removal\":\"mine\"}`, naming the rock under ground nobody has\nwalked. Caught by this spec's own acceptance run, fixed in 4da6c66. A fogged\nendpoint now yields `removal:\"none\", reason:\"unexplored\"` and an unexplored\nnote; a visible endpoint still names the edifice and how it clears.\n\n2. `map-view` fog glyph is `?`, and the pawn band moved.\n\nComment #1 asks for fogged cells to have their own glyph. `?` is the glyph, and\nit forced a second decision: 2.3 used `?` for a Pawn-CATEGORY thing that is not\na Pawn instance (an unreachable case — the things layer filters real pawns out),\nso that case now takes the ordinary item letter.\n\nShould-fix 6 asked for a disjoint pawn band. The band is `? @ $ ! \u0026 ^ ;` —\n`?` fog, `@` colonist, `$` prisoner, `!` hostile, `\u0026` guest/neutral humanlike,\n`^` tame animal, `;` wild animal. **This changes 2.3's shipped glyph table:\nguest/tame/wild were `g`/`d`/`w`, which is exactly why `gold`, `duster` and\n`wood` collided with them.** Enforced, not merely declared: UpperLetter and\nLowerLetter push any def label that would land in the band back out, so a modded\nlabel starting with `^` cannot silently become a tame animal. Thing-to-thing\ncollisions (Table/Tree both `T`) stay allowed and stay disambiguated by the\nlegend — only the pawn/fog band is reserved.\n\nCARRY FORWARD: the glyph table in 2.3's closing comment is now stale, and\nRUNLOG session 2's open item \"catalog.py cross-check before 3.2/3.3\" must be\ndone against THIS band, not that one.\n\n3. `digest.colonists` becomes an object.\n\nThe body says \"colonists_cap + more, mirroring the alert rule\", so the section\nis now `{list, total, more, order}` rather than a bare array, with\n`colonists_cap` as a per-call argument (default 10, 1..200). This is a shape\nchange to 2.1's output. It is in 2.6's own scope, nothing consumes the field yet\n(2.2/2.4 unstarted, rwa unwritten), and leaving it an array would have made\n`more` unattachable.\n\nCapped is not enough on its own: a cap in MapPawns order would hide the downed\ncolonist behind ten healthy ones, which is blocker 2's mistake wearing different\nclothes. So the list is ordered by ATTENTION before it cuts — downed 1000,\nmental 900, bleeding 500, needs-tend 400, sick 200, plus (100 - mood%) — and\ntie-broken by name, so the order is deterministic and rwtest can assert on it.\n`order:\"attention-desc\"` is published so a consumer knows position means urgency\nand not roster order.\n\nCap value 10: measured, not guessed. A colonist line is ~95 bytes; at 20\ncolonists the section is 1902 B and the whole digest 2560 B, outside DESIGN's\n~1-2KB. At 10 the section is 981 B and the digest 1639 B.\n\n4. `find-rect`'s blocker shape.\n\n`rejected` stays the reason-\u003ecount tree 2.3 published (stable field). Added\nalongside it: `blockers`, up to 8 entries sorted by count descending, each\n`{why, def, label, removal, reason, count, at}`, plus `blockers_more`. Keyed by\n(def, why, removal) so one wall type is one line however many candidate rects it\nspoiled, with a sample cell. Cheap, because CheckRect fails fast and so records\nat most one blocker per rejected rect.\n\nThis is the shape comment #1 asks 3.3's `place-layout` preflight to mirror. If\n3.3 wants a different one, say so there and 2.6 follows — the field names are\nnot load-bearing anywhere yet.\n\n5. Cell-level rejections carry the pair too.\n\nA cell rejected for fog, terrain, roof or reachability is not blocked by a\nthing, so it carries `removal:\"none\"` with the reason (`\"unexplored\"` for fog,\notherwise the check's own name). A consumer never has to special-case the\nabsence of the field.\n\n6. MaxSide 61, not radius 29 (blocker 4).\n\nThe body offers both. Making the cap odd is the one that satisfies the\nacceptance line \"`map-view {radius:30}` succeeds\" — capping at 29 would have\nleft the documented-legal radius still illegal, just for a different reason.\n61x61 is ~3.7KB, which is fine: map-view is drill-down, not the digest.\n\n7. Power field set.\n\n`gen_w`, `draw_w`, `battery_days`, `batteries`, `nets_with_generator` added;\n`gain_w`, `stored_wd`, `nets` kept with their 2.1 meanings so nothing that\nalready reads them breaks. `battery_days` is null (not -1, not Infinity) while\ngeneration covers the draw: the batteries are charging and \"days left\" has no\nmeaning. `nets` deliberately still counts the game's own `hasPowerSource` —\nwhich counts a bare battery — with `nets_with_generator` as the strict test\nbeside it, rather than silently redefining a published field.\n\n8. `nearest` publishes what it skipped.\n\n`{\"skipped\": {\"fogged\": N, \"unspawned\": N}}` and `pool`. 2.3 dropped fogged\nthings silently, so \"no medicine\" and \"no medicine you have found yet\" were the\nsame answer.","files":null}]}